export const GUIDE = "PARK: Allen Station Park, along Cottonwood Creek, Allen, Texas. The guide covers a walk of about 0.7 mile one way past the 1874 Old Stone Dam (Allen Water Station, National Register of Historic Places; State Archaeological Landmark).\n\nROUTES: Full creek walk (7 stops, about 0.7 mi one way, 45–60 min, one gravel stop). Short and level (stops 1–4, 0.3 mi one way, all paved, benches at stops 1, 3 and 4). From home (all stories, no walking). Accessible parking and restroom at the trailhead (Stop 1).\n\nSTOPS\nSTOP 1: Welcome to Allen Station Park (Trailhead)\nStory: Allen was built because of the creek just ahead. Before there were neighborhoods here, this was open Blackland Prairie, with farms spreading across deep black clay soil. This guide has seven stops along about two-thirds of a mile of mostly paved trail. Each one has a short story, something to notice with your senses, and details about the path ahead so you can decide what suits you today.\nWhat a visitor sees: You are at the edge of a parking area. A wide concrete trail leads north, into a line of tall trees. The trees mark the path of Cottonwood Creek.\nPause and notice: Before you start, stand or sit still for thirty seconds. Count how many different sounds you can hear. Which ones are made by people, and which by nature?\nAccess (sample values, not yet measured): distance Start; surface Concrete, 10 ft wide; slope Flat; bench Yes, shaded; restroom At trailhead; sound Moderate (traffic); on the step-free route.\n\nSTOP 2: Cottonwood Creek (The reason for everything)\nStory: Cottonwood Creek drains about 19 square miles of Plano, Allen, Parker and McKinney before it joins Rowlett Creek. Volunteers with the Allen Heritage Guild like to say there is always water in Cottonwood Creek, even in a drought. That reliable water is the whole story of this place. Steam locomotives needed to refill every 7 to 10 miles. A creek that never ran dry was worth more to a railroad than almost anything else on the prairie.\nWhat a visitor sees: The trail bends to run beside the creek on your right. The water is a few feet below the path, between muddy banks lined with trees. In most seasons you can hear it before you see it.\nPause and notice: Find the sound of moving water. Close your eyes if you like. Is it a trickle, a rush, or nothing at all today? Water levels change with every rain.\nAccess (sample values, not yet measured): distance 350 ft from Stop 1; surface Concrete; slope Gentle, under 3%; bench No; restroom 350 ft back; sound Water, birds; on the step-free route.\n\nSTOP 3: Shade of the Creekside Forest (Trees that hold the banks)\nStory: Along creeks in North Texas, a narrow band of forest grows where the prairie could not: pecan, cedar elm, hackberry, bur oak and the cottonwoods the creek is named for. Their roots knit the soft banks together and slow floodwater. In November the pecans are dropping and the canopy is thinning, so it is a good month to spot birds. Listen for the sharp chip of a Northern Cardinal or the high cry of a Red-tailed Hawk overhead.\nWhat a visitor sees: Trees close in on both sides and the light gets dimmer. Leaves cover the ground beside the path. Some trunks are wide enough that two people could not reach around them.\nPause and notice: If you can reach a tree safely from the path, touch its bark. Is it rough, smooth, ridged or flaky? Trees that look alike from far away feel very different up close.\nAccess (sample values, not yet measured): distance 500 ft from Stop 2; surface Concrete; slope Flat; bench Yes; restroom 850 ft back; sound Quiet; on the step-free route.\n\nSTOP 4: The Old Stone Dam, 1874 (Allen's oldest landmark)\nStory: In 1874 the Houston and Texas Central Railway was building north from Houston toward Denison. It dammed Cottonwood Creek here with blocks of stone to make a pool, then pumped the water up to a tank beside the tracks. In 1912 the railroad needed more water, so it built a taller concrete dam just downstream. The new pool drowned this one. Then in the 1960s the 1912 dam was breached, and the water drained away to reveal the original stone dam, still standing. Today it is part of the Allen Water Station, listed on the National Register of Historic Places, and it is a State Archaeological Landmark. It is one of the oldest structures in the Dallas–Fort Worth area.\nWhat a visitor sees: From the overlook you look down at a long, low wall of squared stone blocks crossing the creek. Water pools behind it and spills over the top in a thin falls. The stones are dark where the water runs and pale where they are dry.\nPause and notice: Listen to the water going over the dam. Then look for anything living on the stones: moss, a turtle sunning, a heron standing still. People built this, but nature has moved in.\nAccess (sample values, not yet measured): distance 650 ft from Stop 3; surface Concrete to overlook; slope Up to 4% for 80 ft; bench Yes, at overlook; restroom 1,500 ft back; sound Falling water; on the step-free route.\n\nSTOP 5: Water Station Ruins (Pump house and tank)\nStory: A pump house here pushed creek water uphill through pipes to a tall tank. Trains stopped, swung a spout over the engine, and filled up. A small crew of railroad workers kept it all running. The station helped put Allen on the map, sometimes in ways nobody planned. On February 22, 1878, Sam Bass and his gang reportedly robbed the train at the Allen depot, often called the first train robbery in Texas.\nWhat a visitor sees: Off the paved trail, low stone and concrete foundations sit in the grass. They are the remains of the pump house and the base of the old water tank.\nPause and notice: Look at the foundations. Can you tell where the walls stood? Imagine the hiss and smell of a steam engine stopping right here.\nAccess (sample values, not yet measured): distance 300 ft from Stop 4; surface Gravel and grass; slope Up to 8% in places; bench No; restroom 1,800 ft back; sound Quiet; NOT step-free (gravel/grass).\n\nSTOP 6: The 1910 Railroad Bridge (Still carrying the line)\nStory: This bridge was built around 1910, as railroads replaced their early crossings with heavier ones. It is one of the six pieces of the Allen Water Station historic district, along with the dam, the track bed, and the ruins of the tank and pump house. The rail corridor that created Allen in 1872 still runs here. Few places in a fast-growing suburb let you stand where the town's first reason for being is still in plain sight.\nWhat a visitor sees: Ahead, a steel and concrete railroad bridge crosses the creek high above the water. The trail passes beside its supports.\nPause and notice: Feel for vibration if a train passes. If it's quiet, look up at the underside of the bridge for swallow or phoebe nests.\nAccess (sample values, not yet measured): distance 450 ft from Stop 4 (paved detour); surface Concrete; slope Flat; bench No; restroom 1,950 ft back; sound Occasional trains; on the step-free route.\n\nSTOP 7: Prairie Edge (Less than 1% remains)\nStory: Everything you have walked past grew out of this: the Blackland Prairie, a tallgrass prairie on deep black clay that once ran from near San Antonio to the Red River. Its soil was so rich that almost all of it was plowed for farms. Less than 1% of the original prairie remains. Grasses like little bluestem turn copper-red in the fall. Spring brings Indian blanket and other wildflowers. Small restored patches like this one feed pollinators and birds, and give people a place to slow down.\nWhat a visitor sees: The trees open up. In front of you is a wide, sunny stretch of tall grass and seed heads, moving in the wind. The sky feels bigger here.\nPause and notice: Last stop. Take one slow breath in, and one slow breath out. What is one thing you noticed today that you would have walked past before?\nAccess (sample values, not yet measured): distance 400 ft from Stop 6; surface Concrete; mowed grass beyond; slope Flat; bench Yes, sunny; restroom 2,350 ft back; sound Wind, insects; on the step-free route.\n\nFIELD GUIDE\nPecan (Carya illinoinensis) — plants\nThe Texas state tree, and one of the tallest trees along the creek. Look for: Long leaves made of 9 to 17 narrow leaflets. In fall, smooth brown nuts drop from split green husks. Other senses: Feel for pecans underfoot from October to December. Crushed leaves smell spicy and resinous. Why it matters: Squirrels, crows and jays bury pecans and forget some, which is how new pecan trees get planted along the creek.\nNear stops 1, 3; seasons: fall, winter.\n\nCedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia) — plants\nOne of the most widespread native trees in Texas, and common along North Texas creeks. Look for: Small oval leaves, about an inch long, with toothed edges. Twigs often have corky 'wings.' Leaves turn yellow in late fall. Other senses: Rub a leaf between your fingers: the top feels like fine sandpaper. Why it matters: It handles both flooding and drought, which is exactly what a North Texas creek bank delivers.\nNear stops 3, 4; seasons: spring, summer, fall.\n\nEastern Cottonwood (Populus deltoides) — plants\nThe tree Cottonwood Creek is named for. Fast-growing and thirsty, it lives where roots can reach water. Look for: Triangle-shaped leaves with coarse teeth and a flat leaf stalk. Deeply furrowed grey bark on old trees. Other senses: Listen in a breeze. The flat stalks make the leaves flutter and clatter like light rain. Why it matters: In late spring its seeds float away on white cotton fluff, which is where the name comes from.\nNear stops 2, 3; seasons: spring, summer, fall.\n\nBur Oak (Quercus macrocarpa) — plants\nA slow-growing giant. Some bur oaks in Collin County are thought to be more than 200 years old. Look for: Large leaves with deep lobes near the base and a wide, wavy top. Its acorns are huge, with a shaggy fringed cap. Other senses: Pick up an acorn cap if you find one. The fringe feels like a tiny bristly hat. Why it matters: It produces the largest acorns of any North American oak, an important fall food for deer, squirrels and turkeys.\nNear stops 3; seasons: fall.\n\nLittle Bluestem (Schizachyrium scoparium) — plants\nOne of the main grasses of the Blackland Prairie. Easy to spot in fall. Look for: Clumps of upright stems, blue-green in summer and copper-red from October through winter. Fluffy silver seed tufts. Other senses: The seed tufts catch low sun and glow. Brush your hand over the tops: soft, not scratchy. Why it matters: Its roots reach several feet down, which helps prairie soil hold water and stay put.\nNear stops 7; seasons: fall, winter.\n\nMaximilian Sunflower (Helianthus maximiliani) — plants\nA tall native prairie sunflower that blooms when most other wildflowers are finished. Look for: Stalks 3 to 10 feet tall, lined with narrow leaves and topped with many golden flowers stacked up the stem. Blooms September and October. Other senses: Watch for bees and butterflies working the flowers on warm fall afternoons. Why it matters: Its seeds feed goldfinches, sparrows and other birds into winter.\nNear stops 7; seasons: fall.\n\nIndian Blanket (Gaillardia pulchella) — plants\nA bright prairie wildflower that paints sunny ground red and yellow from late spring through summer. Look for: Daisy-like flowers 1 to 2 inches wide: red petals tipped in yellow around a dark red center. Other senses: After the petals fall, the round seed head feels like a soft burr. Why it matters: A favorite of native bees, and it reseeds itself readily in disturbed ground.\nNear stops 7; seasons: spring, summer.\n\nPoison Ivy (Toxicodendron radicans) — plants — CAUTION: do not touch\nCommon on creek trees and fence lines. Look, but don't touch. Look for: Leaves of three: two side leaflets and a middle one on a longer stalk. Can be a ground cover, a shrub or a hairy vine up a tree. Turns red in fall. Other senses: There is no safe way to identify it by touch. Its oil causes an itchy rash, even from bare winter vines. Why it matters: Its white berries are an important winter food for many birds, which is why it grows where birds perch.\nNear stops 2, 3, 5; seasons: spring, summer, fall, winter.\n\nGreat Blue Heron (Ardea herodias) — animals\nThe tallest heron in North America, often standing perfectly still in the shallows below the dam. Look for: Blue-grey, about 4 feet tall, with long legs, an S-curved neck and a dagger-like yellow bill. Flies with its neck tucked in. Other senses: If startled it takes off with slow, deep wingbeats and a hoarse, croaking call. Why it matters: It hunts fish, frogs and crayfish by waiting, then striking fast. A heron here is a sign the creek has food in it.\nNear stops 2, 4; seasons: spring, summer, fall, winter.\n\nRed-tailed Hawk (Buteo jamaicensis) — animals\nThe most common large hawk in North America. Watch the sky over open ground and the rail line. Look for: Broad wings and a short, fan-shaped tail that is brick-red on adults. Pale chest with a band of dark streaks across the belly. Other senses: Its raspy, descending 'keee-arr' scream is the sound movies often use for eagles. Why it matters: It hunts rabbits, rats and mice from perches and in slow, soaring circles.\nNear stops 6, 7; seasons: spring, summer, fall, winter.\n\nNorthern Cardinal (Cardinalis cardinalis) — animals\nA year-round resident. The bright red male is easy to see when the leaves drop. Look for: Males are red all over with a black face and a pointed crest. Females are warm tan with red in the wings and tail. Both have a thick orange bill. Other senses: A sharp metallic 'chip' from the bushes, or a loud whistled 'cheer, cheer, cheer' song. Why it matters: Its heavy bill cracks seeds. Cardinals stay in pairs and sing to hold their territory all year.\nNear stops 1, 3; seasons: spring, summer, fall, winter.\n\nFox Squirrel (Sciurus niger) — animals\nThe big rusty-orange squirrel of Texas parks, busiest in fall when the pecans drop. Look for: Grizzled grey-brown back, orange belly and an enormous bushy tail. Larger than the grey squirrels of the eastern U.S. Other senses: Listen for scolding chatter and tail-flicking, or the scratch of claws on bark. Why it matters: Each squirrel buries hundreds of nuts in fall. The ones it never digs up become the next generation of trees.\nNear stops 1, 3; seasons: spring, summer, fall, winter.\n\nCoyote (Canis latrans) — animals\nCoyotes live along creek corridors across Dallas–Fort Worth, Allen included. Usually heard more than seen. Look for: Like a lean, medium-sized dog with tawny-grey fur, pointed ears, a narrow snout and a bushy tail carried low. Other senses: At dusk and night, a burst of yips and howls. During the day, look for tracks in creek-bank mud. Why it matters: They help control rodents and rabbits. Keep dogs leashed and give coyotes space; they normally avoid people.\nNear stops 5, 7; seasons: spring, summer, fall, winter.\n\nEastern Cottontail (Sylvilagus floridanus) — animals\nCommon on grassy trail edges, especially early morning and evening. Look for: Brown-grey rabbit with long ears and a round white tail that flashes when it runs. Other senses: Often freezes in place when you approach. Walk slowly and quietly to watch it longer. Why it matters: It's food for hawks, owls and coyotes, which makes it a key link in the park's food web.\nNear stops 1, 7; seasons: spring, summer, fall, winter.\n\nRed-eared Slider (Trachemys scripta elegans) — critters\nThe turtle most often seen sunning on logs and on the stones of the dam. Look for: Olive-green shell, yellow-striped skin and a red patch behind each eye. Other senses: Approach and you'll hear a 'plop' as it slides into the water, which is how it got its name. Why it matters: Turtles bask to warm up and dry their shells. On warm November days they're still out; in winter they rest on the creek bottom.\nNear stops 2, 4; seasons: spring, summer, fall.\n\nMonarch Butterfly (Danaus plexippus) — critters\nMonarchs pass through North Texas each fall on their way to mountain forests in Mexico. Look for: Large orange wings with black veins and a black border dotted with white. Glides with wings held in a shallow V. Other senses: Watch for them nectaring on late flowers like sunflowers. Peak passage here is usually October. Why it matters: Caterpillars eat only milkweed. Planting native milkweed and fall flowers helps them make the trip.\nNear stops 7; seasons: spring, fall.\n\nGreen Anole (Anolis carolinensis) — critters\nA small native lizard found on tree trunks, fences and shrubs on warm days. Look for: Slim, 5 to 8 inches long including the tail. Bright green, but it can turn brown in minutes. Males flash a pink throat fan. Other senses: Look for quick movement on sunny bark, or a male bobbing its head like it's doing push-ups. Why it matters: It eats spiders, flies and crickets, and is a meal for birds and snakes.\nNear stops 1, 3; seasons: spring, summer, fall.\n\nCommon Green Darner (Anax junius) — critters\nOne of the largest dragonflies in North America, patrolling over the creek and open grass. Look for: About 3 inches long, with a green thorax, a long blue or purple-brown body and four clear wings. Other senses: Listen for the dry rustle of its wings as it zips past at head height. Why it matters: Like monarchs, many green darners migrate south in fall. They catch mosquitoes and other insects in flight.\nNear stops 2, 4; seasons: spring, summer, fall.\n\nRed Imported Fire Ant (Solenopsis invicta) — critters — CAUTION: do not touch\nA non-native ant common in mowed grass. Their sting burns and can blister. Look for: Loose, fluffy dirt mounds with no opening on top, often popping up after rain. Small reddish-brown ants. Other senses: If you sit on grass, check the ground first. If ants climb on you, brush them off quickly; don't shake. Why it matters: Arrived in the U.S. in the 1930s and spread across Texas. They compete with native ants and can harm ground-nesting wildlife.\nNear stops 1, 7; seasons: spring, summer, fall.\n\nSAFETY: Stay on the trail. Most snakes are harmless but copperheads live in North Texas leaf litter. Watch for poison ivy and fire ant mounds. Give wildlife room, never feed animals, keep dogs leashed. Leave plants, nuts and stones in place.";
